title: file panel
tags: Panel [[Panel types]]
kind: reference
aliases: file viewer preview zoom gif
summary: A preview of the selected file — image, video, audio, PDF, text — or a folder as a grid of thumbnails, with zoom, a media player's keys and a way up.

Open it with `panel:set type file` (<<key "panel:set type file" "t f">>). It shows
the file the workspace has selected, and follows the selection: move the cursor in
[[metarecord-list panel]] or [[file-manager panel]] beside it and the preview changes
([[Cross-panel selection]]). The file need not be tracked. With nothing selected it
says ''No file selected''.

Its path is in the bar at the top. A metarecord that has several paths gets a
drop-down there, to choose which one is previewed.

!! What is shown

What a file is shown as depends on what it ''is'', read from its first bytes — not on
its name. A video without an extension is a video; `clip.ts` holding an MPEG
transport stream is a video and `main.ts` holding TypeScript is text.

|!File |!Shown as |
|image (PNG, JPEG, WebP, BMP, AVIF, ICO, SVG) |the image |
|GIF |its first frame; ''animate'' in the toolbar (`file:toggle gif-animation`) plays it |
|video |its poster frame, until you ask to play it ([[Media preview]]) |
|audio |a type glyph, until you ask to play it ([[Media preview]]) |
|PDF |one page at a time ([[Document preview]]) |
|a folder |a grid of thumbnails (below) |
|anything else |text when it is text — its first 256 KiB, then `… (truncated preview)` — else ''no preview available for this format'' |

Text has no signature to read, so it is what is left once the file is none of the
above. A file whose extension is in `text-extensions` (`txt`, `md`, `json`, `rs`…)
is shown as text whatever it contains, which is how a UTF-16 file — full of the zero
bytes the content test rejects — still previews; any other is shown as text when its
content looks like text. That list, the size read and the zoom's step and bounds are
settings of `[panel-defaults.file]` ([[GUI configuration]]).

An image format the web view cannot draw (TIFF, HEIF, a camera's raw file) is not
counted as an image, and gets ''no preview available''.

A file on a volume that is not mounted says which volume, instead of failing to load
([[Unmounted volumes in the GUI]]).

!! A folder

A folder is a grid of tiles, folders first: an image is its own thumbnail, a video
its poster frame, a GIF a still, a PDF its first page, the rest a type glyph. The
tiles are loaded 150 at a time (`[page-size]`), the next ones on scroll; the footer
gives `shown/total`.

Clicking a tile goes into it — a subfolder's grid, a file's preview — without
changing the selection. <<key "file:back" Backspace>>, or ''←'' in the path bar,
comes back up. It also works on a plain preview: it then shows the folder the file
is in, so the panel browses upwards from wherever it is. The panel follows the
selection again once the walk is back on the selected path, or as soon as the
selection changes.

!! Zoom

For an image, a document page and a video once its player is loaded. The toolbar
has the same four as buttons.

|!Key |!Does |
|<<key "file:zoom in" "+">> / <<key "file:zoom out" "-">> |one step larger, smaller (×1.25) |
|<<key "file:zoom fit" "=">> |fit the panel, proportions kept — the default |
|<<key "file:zoom reset" "0">> |the original size |

A manual zoom sizes the picture from its own pixels, between 5 % and 4000 %, and the
panel scrolls when it overflows; the first step starts from what ''fit'' was showing.
The zoom is kept from one file to the next. In `keybindings.toml` the `+` key is
written `plus`, `+` being what joins the keys of a combination ([[Keybindings]]).

!! Walking a list from the preview

<<key "metarecord-list:next" ↓>> / <<key "metarecord-list:prev" ↑>> and
<<key "metarecord-list:first" Home>> / <<key "metarecord-list:last" End>> are bound
here to the ''list's'' commands: they move the cursor of the workspace's
[[metarecord-list panel]], and the preview follows. That is how a folder of pictures
is flipped through without leaving the preview — plain keybindings, to change like
the others.

!! Right click

In a workspace that has a repository, a right click offers the file's actions,
tracked or not —
''Open with…'', the clipboard, ''Rename…'', ''Duplicate'', ''Copy full path'', ''Move to
trash'' ([[Context menus]]). They act on the file ''shown'', which after a click on a
tile or a step up is not the selected one.

A tracked file the selection brings here is recorded as viewed, for the
[[recent panel]].
